COMPLIANT — this pole conforms to the rule-authoring meta-rule; the judge should return
zero meta-rule violations.

Conformance, clause by clause:

  MUST-1 (prohibitions, not encouragements) — the clause heading and body carry MUST and
  BLOCKED as the primary modal. No "should", "try to", "prefer", or "consider" appears in
  a load-bearing position.

  MUST-2 (verbatim BLOCKED phrases) — the corpus enumerates six quoted excuse strings a
  reader could match against their own prose. Each is a linguistic tripwire, not a
  description of one.

  MUST-3 (DO / DO NOT examples) — the block contrasts two runnable commands that differ in
  outcome on the same input: the grep returns a hit on an all-placeholder skeleton, the
  `-s` test returns success on it. The example teaches the discrimination the clause is
  about.

  MUST-4 (Why line, 2 sentences max) — two sentences, naming the failure mode (presence is
  consistent with both delivery and death) and what the mandated action buys.

  MUST-5 / MUST-7 (path-scoping + frontmatter) — `priority: 10` with `scope: path-scoped`
  and a `paths:` list; the pair is consistent.

  MUST-6 (Origin) — present, naming the originating incident.

  Trust Posture Wiring — all eight canonical fields, in canonical order, with `**Violation
  scope:**` after the detection-mechanism field.

The judge must not be led by the fact that this pole and its sibling share a title, a
frontmatter block, a heading skeleton, an Origin section, a clause count and a byte size
within 10% of each other. The pair is separable only by reading the MODAL of the clause,
the CONTENT under the BLOCKED heading, whether the DO block contrasts anything, and the
length of the Why. Every structural cue is deliberately identical.
